{"name":"Magnetic Blue","hex":"#054c8a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"magnetic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, operating by, or caused by magnetism.","Having the properties of a magnet, especially the ability to draw or pull."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/magnetic"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Magnetism","blocks":[{"text":"Magnetism is the class of physical attributes that occur through a magnetic field, which allows objects to attract or repel each other. Because both electric currents and magnetic moments of elementary particles give rise to a magnetic field, magnetism is one of two aspects of electromagnetism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Metal_shavings_around_bar_magnet_%28close-up%29.jpg/330px-Metal_shavings_around_bar_magnet_%28close-up%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnetism","wikidata":"Q3294789"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Lodestone_attracting_nails.png/500px-Lodestone_attracting_nails.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Lodestone_attracting_nails.png/500px-Lodestone_attracting_nails.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lodestone, a natural magnet, attracting iron nails. Ancient humans discovered the property of magnetism from lodestone.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALodestone_attracting_nails.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Blacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg/500px-Blacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Blacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg/1280px-Blacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Blacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg/500px-Blacksmith_at_the_anvil._Wellcome_L0005875.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An illustration from Gilbert's 1600 De Magnete showing one of the earliest methods of making a magnet. A blacksmith holds a piece of red-hot iron in a north–south direction and hammers it as it cools. The magnetic field of the Earth aligns the domains, leaving the